  "subtitle-final": [
    "The 2026 FIFA World Cup begins June 11 across North America, with the final scheduled for July 19 at MetLife Stadium. Spain enters as the narrow favorite to reach the final, buoyed by its status as reigning European champions and strong recent results, though forward Lamine Yamal\u2019s hamstring recovery timeline creates uncertainty for early matches. France remains a close co-favorite, relying on its deep squad despite reported concerns over defender William Saliba\u2019s availability. Argentina, the defending champions, England, and Brazil round out the leading contenders, each with experienced cores but facing questions around form and fitness. Multiple high-profile injuries across several nations, including key absences for Germany and Canada, add volatility heading into group play, where path and matchups will quickly shape advancement odds."
